  "query": "Conduct comprehensive market sizing and methodology research for this market:\n\n**Industry:** Medical Devices & MedTech / Wearable & Implantable Biosensing; Neurotechnology; Continuous Glucose Monitoring\n**Solution Category:** Flexible Substrate Components and Modules for Medical Devices (CGMS, Neurostimulation, BCI)\n**Geography:** North America, Europe (initial); Global (expansion)\n**Business Model:** B2B Component Supply / CDMO\n**Target Buyer:** Enterprise (Medical Device OEMs, MedTech System Integrators, BCI/CGMS Startups)\n**Problem Context:** LG Innotek (LGIT) possesses advanced CoF and flexible PCB substrate manufacturing capabilities but has no established footprint in the medical device market. The company faces the strategic challenge of determining whether its 10\u00b5m fine-pattern substrate technology constitutes defensible, monetizable differentiation in high-value medical applications (CGMS, neurostimulation, BCI), and how to navigate value chain positioning, OEM gating, and regulatory certification barriers to achieve superior margin profiles versus its commodity substrate business.\n**Market Terms:** flexible electrode substrate for medical devices, CoF-based medical device components, continuous glucose monitoring substrate, neural electrode array substrate, BCI flexible circuit substrate, neurostimulation electrode components, wearable biosensor substrate, implantable flexible substrate, fine-pattern biocompatible electrode, medical FPCB components, CGMS patch electrode technology, stretchable substrate medical applications, high-density multi-channel electrode substrate, medical device CDMO substrate supply, thin-film wearable biosensor components\n**Known Competitors:** Medtronic, Abbott, Murata, Linxes, Jabil, Integer, Flex\n**Known Segments:** CGMS OEMs (e.g., Abbott, Dexcom, Medtronic), Neurostimulation device manufacturers (e.g., Medtronic, Boston Scientific), BCI system companies (early-stage startups, e.g., Neuralink, Synchron, Echo), Medical device CDMOs and contract manufacturers, MedTech system integrators requiring electrode substrates, Big Tech companies with medical device investment interest (Apple, Google), Academic and clinical research institutions requiring electrode components, Strategic investors and M&A acquirers in MedTech component space\n\n**Venture Claims to Verify:**\n  - CGMS market described as continuing to expand due to growing demand for daily health monitoring\n  - Neurostimulation and BCI need increasing due to rising cardiovascular and neurological disease prevalence\n  - North America seeing strong investment in brain-control and brain-sensing innovation\n  - Early-stage and growth-stage BCI startups attracting significant capital\n  - Reference investment benchmark: ~USD 35 million (recent LGIT investment)\n\n\nSearch for: \"Flexible Substrate Components and Modules for Medical Devices (CGMS, Neurostimulation, BCI) market size North America, Europe (initial); Global (expansion)\", \"Medical Devices & MedTech Wearable & Implantable Biosensing; Neurotechnology; Continuous Glucose Monitoring TAM\", \"Flexible Substrate Components and Modules for Medical Devices (CGMS, Neurostimulation, BCI) market growth forecast\", \"Flexible Substrate Components and Modules for Medical Devices (CGMS, Neurostimulation, BCI) market methodology\", \"Flexible Substrate Components and Modules for Medical Devices (CGMS, Neurostimulation, BCI) CAGR\", \"Flexible Substrate Components and Modules for Medical Devices (CGMS, Neurostimulation, BCI) market revenue\".\n\nResearch and report on ALL of the following. For every data point, cite the specific source and publication date.\n\n**A. Market Sizing Data**\n1. **Named TAM estimates**: Individual market-size figures from named third-party sources with year, scope, and source attached. Report ALL estimates found, not just one.\n2. **TAM range**: Synthesize the low, midpoint, and high estimates from all sources found. Make uncertainty explicit.\n3. **Historical market size series**: At least 3-5 years of historical data. Note data gaps.\n4. **Projected market size series**: Forward estimates with forecast horizon. Include multiple projections if available.\n5. **Historical CAGR by geography or segment**: Where available, break out growth rates by region or subsegment.\n6. **Projected CAGR by geography or segment**: Forecast growth rates by region or subsegment. Identify where future demand concentrates.\n\n**B. Sizing Methodology & Confidence**\n7. **Sizing methodology per source**: Whether each source uses top-down, bottom-up, value-based, shipment-based, or spend-based methods. Compare rigor across sources.\n8. **Bottom-up demand-unit proxies**: Public counts for buyers, users, seats, facilities, transactions, or assets that can anchor bottom-up sizing. Search for government censuses, industry directories, public databases.\n9. **Unit price / spend / value-per-unit proxies**: Benchmarks for spend per account, per seat, per transaction that convert volume into dollar market size.\n10. **Hard-data anchor points**: Official data from SEC filings, government statistics, regulator databases, or standards bodies that serve as high-confidence anchors.\n11. **Source credibility metadata**: For each source, note recency, independence, transparency, sample quality, potential sponsor bias, and methodological caveats.\n12. **Pricing trend / ASP trajectory**: Average selling price trends over time \u2014 is pricing rising, falling, or compressing by segment?\n\nPrioritize data from the last 24 months. If only older data exists, note this explicitly. Distinguish between primary research, analyst estimates, and government statistics. Flag significant variance between sources.",
